model=poly:2: the student types a function of that family, graded; pts= is what it is worth and fncolor= colours their curve.

@graph(ask=fit, model=poly:2, pts=2, fncolor=#cc0033, points="1,2; 2,4; 3,9; 4,16")

model=poly:3: the student types a function of that family, graded; pts= is what it is worth and fncolor= colours their curve.

@graph(ask=fit, model=poly:3, pts=2, fncolor=#cc0033, points="1,2; 2,4; 3,9; 4,16")

model=exp: the student types a function of that family, graded; pts= is what it is worth and fncolor= colours their curve.

@graph(ask=fit, model=exp, pts=2, fncolor=#cc0033, points="1,2; 2,4; 3,9; 4,16")

model=power: the student types a function of that family, graded; pts= is what it is worth and fncolor= colours their curve.

@graph(ask=fit, model=power, pts=2, fncolor=#cc0033, points="1,2; 2,4; 3,9; 4,16")

model=log: the student types a function of that family, graded; pts= is what it is worth and fncolor= colours their curve.

@graph(ask=fit, model=log, pts=2, fncolor=#cc0033, points="1,2; 2,4; 3,9; 4,16")

ask=plot just plots, with nothing graded.

@graph(ask=plot, points="0,1; 1,3; 2,5")

cluster=kmeans:3 colours the points by that many clusters.

@graph(type=scatter, x=x, y=y, cluster=kmeans:3)
